Subject: [PATCH 0014] pymalloc blocks as Sublet child lifetimes (CDERIVE/CREVOKE)
Upstream: CPython 3.13.7
Upstream-Archive: https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.13.7/Python-3.13.7.tgz
Upstream-SHA256: 6c9d80839cfa20024f34d9a6dd31ae2a9cd97ff5e980e969209746037a5153b2
Build-Variants: capstone64 virtual application (musl), CPY_SUBLET=1
Apply-From: upstream source root after 0009, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1
Needs: -I capstone/runtime/include (capability.h) on the compile line

The protection is two instructions inserted where obmalloc hands a block out
and takes it back (docs/design/virtual-capstone/isa.md):

- new_arena derives a child of the arena the system allocator returned
  (arena_life). The child carries MANAGE over its own children. Freeing the
  arena revokes the system allocator's lifetime of it, and this child lies
  below that, so arena release needs no code of its own.
- pymalloc_alloc returns a child of arena_life bounded to the request
  instead of its own pointer.
- pymalloc_free revokes that child (CREVOKE), which faults on a reference
  that is not a live block of this arena, and then continues with obmalloc's
  own pointer at the same address. That pointer is derived from 0009's
  arena_ptr, which outlives every block.
- pymalloc_realloc replaces the client's child when the block stays, and
  copies only the issued length when it moves.

A client's pointer is bounded to its block, so obmalloc cannot reach the
pool header by aligning it down. The radix tree's leaf therefore also
records which arena its tails belong to (arena_hi, arena_lo), and
arena_map_arena finds a client pointer's arena by address.

Requests over 512 bytes are not touched: they go to the system allocator as
upstream, which on the virtual profile bounds and retires them itself.

